BANK.push(
{"id": "m1", "d": 3, "q": "Two identical VMs run 24x7 for a month, one in East US and one in Brazil South. The Brazil South VM costs noticeably more. Which cost factor explains this?", "o": ["Resource type", "Geography (region)", "Subscription type", "Marketplace licensing"], "a": [1], "e": "Prices for the same resource vary by region due to local costs like power, labor, and taxes. Geography is one of the main cost factors in Azure."},
{"id": "m2", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about Azure bandwidth charges is generally TRUE?", "o": ["Data into Azure (ingress) is generally free; data out (egress) is generally charged", "Ingress and egress are both free as long as the traffic stays within one geography", "Ingress is charged per GB, while egress to the internet is free for the first 1 TB", "All traffic between Azure regions is free, but ingress from the internet is charged"], "a": [0], "e": "Inbound data transfer is generally free. Outbound data transfer from Azure to the internet and between regions is generally billed, with pricing based on billing zones."},
{"id": "m3", "d": 3, "q": "A team replicates data from a VM in West Europe to a database in East US every hour. Which cost will this create that wouldn't exist if both were in the same region?", "o": ["Inter-region data transfer charges", "Resource group transfer fees", "Management group licensing fees", "Tag processing charges"], "a": [0], "e": "Data moving between Azure regions is billed as data transfer. Keeping chatty components in the same region avoids it."},
{"id": "m4", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO factors directly affect the cost of an Azure VM? (Choose two.)", "o": ["The VM size (vCPU and memory)", "The region where it runs", "The name of the resource group", "The number of tags applied"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "VM size, region, OS licensing, runtime, and attached resources affect cost. Resource group names and tags don't."},
{"id": "m5", "d": 3, "q": "A company deploys a Marketplace image of a third-party firewall appliance. Where do the software license costs appear?", "o": ["They're included free with Azure for Marketplace images", "On the Azure bill, alongside the Azure infrastructure costs", "Only on a separate invoice sent by Microsoft Learn each month", "They're charged to the publisher, not to the customer using it"], "a": [1], "e": "Azure Marketplace solutions may include third-party software charges billed through your Azure invoice in addition to the underlying Azure resources."},
{"id": "m6", "d": 3, "q": "An app's cost spikes when a misconfigured loop calls a paid API millions of times. Which cost factor is MOST directly responsible?", "o": ["Consumption", "Geography", "Subscription type", "Maintenance"], "a": [0], "e": "Consumption (usage) drives cost in a pay-as-you-go model. More calls, more compute time, or more storage means a bigger bill."},
{"id": "m7", "d": 3, "q": "Why can 'maintenance' be a cost factor in Azure?", "o": ["Microsoft charges extra each month for patching the platform hosts", "Resources left behind after a project, like disks and IPs, keep billing", "Every subscription needs a paid maintenance contract to stay supported", "Planned maintenance windows are billed at a premium compute rate"], "a": [1], "e": "Microsoft lists maintenance as a cost factor because resources left behind after projects (disks, IPs, VMs) keep accruing charges. Regular cleanup controls costs."},
{"id": "m8", "d": 3, "q": "A company uses an Azure free account. Which statement about subscription type as a cost factor is TRUE?", "o": ["Free accounts have no usage limits as long as you stay in one region", "Some subscription types include allowances or credits that change what you pay", "Subscription type has no effect on cost, since all prices are the same everywhere", "Free accounts can never create paid resources, even after upgrading to pay-as-you-go"], "a": [1], "e": "Subscription types like free accounts or Visual Studio subscriptions include credits or allowances. Free accounts can be upgraded to pay-as-you-go."},
{"id": "m9", "d": 3, "q": "A project manager needs an estimated monthly cost for a proposed design of 4 VMs, a SQL database, and 10 TB of blob storage before anything is deployed. Which tool fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Pricing Calculator", "Microsoft Cost Management cost analysis", "Azure Advisor", "Azure Service Health"], "a": [0], "e": "The Pricing Calculator estimates the cost of services you plan to deploy. Cost Management analyzes spending on resources you already have; Advisor recommends optimizations for existing resources."},
{"id": "m10", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about the Azure Pricing Calculator is TRUE?", "o": ["It needs an active Azure subscription and reads your current usage to build estimates", "It gives estimates, and you can change region, tier, and purchase options to compare", "It shows your actual invoice for last month, broken down by service and resource", "It deploys the resources you configure so you can test real costs before committing"], "a": [1], "e": "The Pricing Calculator is free, doesn't need a subscription, and gives estimates for configurations you choose. It doesn't deploy resources or show actual bills."},
{"id": "m11", "d": 3, "q": "A CFO wants to compare the cost of running the current on-premises datacenter against running the same workloads in Azure, including power, cooling, and labor. Which tool is designed for this comparison?", "o": ["Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) Calculator", "Azure Pricing Calculator", "Azure Advisor cost recommendations", "Microsoft Cost Management budgets"], "a": [0], "e": "The TCO Calculator compares on-premises infrastructure costs (including power, cooling, labor) with Azure. The Pricing Calculator estimates Azure-only costs for a design."},
{"id": "m12", "d": 3, "q": "In the Azure Pricing Calculator, an architect switches a VM from pay-as-you-go to a three-year reservation and the estimate drops sharply. What does this demonstrate?", "o": ["Purchase options affect cost estimates", "Reservations remove storage costs", "The calculator reflects actual invoice discounts already applied", "Region changes are required to see reservation pricing"], "a": [0], "e": "The Pricing Calculator lets you compare purchase models like pay-as-you-go, reservations, and savings plans to see how commitments change cost."},
{"id": "m13", "d": 3, "q": "Which Microsoft Cost Management feature shows where money was spent, broken down by resource group, service, location, or tag?", "o": ["Budgets", "Cost analysis", "Pricing Calculator", "Azure Advisor score"], "a": [1], "e": "Cost analysis lets you explore and break down actual and forecasted costs by many dimensions. Budgets set thresholds and alerts."},
{"id": "m14", "d": 3, "q": "A team sets a $5,000 monthly budget on a subscription. Spending hits $5,200. What happens by default?", "o": ["Azure shuts down all resources in the subscription until the next month starts", "Azure sends alerts to the configured recipients, but resources keep running", "Azure blocks creation of any new resources in the subscription for the month", "Azure moves the subscription's resources to cheaper tiers automatically"], "a": [1], "e": "Budgets trigger alerts at defined thresholds. By default, they don't stop resources. You can connect budget alerts to action groups (for example, to run automation), but that's extra setup."},
{"id": "m15", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO are types of cost alerts in Microsoft Cost Management? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Budget alerts", "Credit alerts", "Service Health alerts", "Resource Health alerts"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Cost Management alerts include budget alerts, credit alerts (for Azure Prepayment/credit usage), and department spending quota alerts. Service and Resource Health alerts are monitoring, not cost alerts."},
{"id": "m16", "d": 3, "q": "A team wants budget alerts to trigger an automation that deallocates dev VMs when spending reaches 90% of the budget. What makes this possible?", "o": ["Budgets can call action groups that run automation", "Budgets automatically deallocate VMs at 90%", "Azure Policy can read budget thresholds and stop VMs", "Resource locks prevent overspending"], "a": [0], "e": "Budget alerts can be tied to action groups, which can trigger runbooks, Logic Apps, or functions to take action. Budgets don't stop resources on their own."},
{"id": "m17", "d": 3, "q": "Finance wants monthly costs split by department across 20 subscriptions where each department's resources are mixed together in shared resource groups. What is the MOST effective approach?", "o": ["Tag resources with a department and group costs by that tag in cost analysis", "Create a separate subscription for every resource so each has its own invoice", "Rename every resource with a department prefix and filter costs by name", "Move every resource into its own resource group and report by group name"], "a": [0], "e": "Tags let you categorize resources across resource groups and subscriptions, and cost analysis can group and filter by tag. This is the standard way to do chargeback when resources share containers."},
{"id": "m18", "d": 3, "q": "A tag 'CostCenter=1234' is applied to a resource group. A VM is then created in that group. Does the VM have the tag?", "o": ["Yes, tags on a resource group are always inherited by resources created in it", "No, tags aren't inherited by default, but Azure Policy can add or inherit them", "Yes, but only when the VM is deployed to the same region as the resource group", "No, because tags can only be applied at the subscription level, not to groups"], "a": [1], "e": "Resources don't inherit tags from their resource group or subscription by default. Azure Policy (for example, 'Inherit a tag from the resource group') can apply them automatically."},
{"id": "m19", "d": 3, "q": "A company requires every new resource to have an 'Owner' tag. Deployments without the tag must be rejected. Which approach fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Policy with a deny effect for resources missing the tag", "A ReadOnly resource lock on each resource group in the subscription", "An RBAC custom role that only includes the tagging permissions", "A budget alert that fires whenever untagged resources show up"], "a": [0], "e": "Azure Policy can require tags and deny non-compliant deployments. Locks prevent change/delete, RBAC grants permissions, and budgets track cost."},
{"id": "m20", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO are common purposes of tags in Azure? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Cost management and chargeback", "Operations management, such as identifying workload owners", "Enforcing network isolation between resources", "Granting users permissions to resources"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Tags help with resource organization, cost management, operations, security classification, governance, and automation. They don't isolate networks or grant permissions."},
{"id": "m21", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about tags is TRUE?", "o": ["Each resource can have an unlimited number of tags, but each name must be unique", "Each resource, resource group, or subscription can have up to 50 name/value pairs", "Tag values must be unique across the subscription so cost reports don't overlap", "Tag values are encrypted at rest, so they're a safe place to store secrets"], "a": [1], "e": "Most resources, resource groups, and subscriptions support up to 50 tag name/value pairs. Tags are plain text metadata; never put secrets in them."},
{"id": "m22", "d": 3, "q": "A security team wants a unified view of where sensitive data lives across Azure SQL, Azure Storage, on-premises SQL Server, and Amazon S3, with classification and lineage. Which service fits BEST?", "o": ["Microsoft Purview", "Azure Policy", "Microsoft Defender for Cloud", "Azure Monitor"], "a": [0], "e": "Microsoft Purview provides data governance: automated discovery, classification, a data map, catalog, and lineage across Azure, on-premises, multicloud, and SaaS sources."},
{"id": "m23", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement BEST distinguishes Microsoft Purview from Azure Policy?", "o": ["Purview governs data (discovery, classification); Policy governs resource settings", "Purview governs resource settings; Azure Policy discovers and classifies your data", "They're the same service, with Purview being the newer name for Azure Policy", "Purview only works on-premises, while Azure Policy only works inside Azure"], "a": [0], "e": "Purview focuses on data governance, risk, and compliance. Azure Policy evaluates and enforces rules on resource properties and configuration."},
{"id": "m24", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO capabilities are part of Microsoft Purview? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Data map and data catalog", "Data lineage tracking", "Virtual machine autoscaling", "Network traffic filtering"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Purview provides a data map, data catalog, lineage, classification, and risk and compliance solutions. Autoscaling and traffic filtering are compute and network features."},
{"id": "m25", "d": 3, "q": "A company wants to ensure no one can create VMs larger than a certain size anywhere in the production management group. Which tool fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Policy using allowed VM SKUs with a deny effect", "Azure RBAC Reader role assigned to all non-admins", "A CanNotDelete lock on the production subscriptions", "A budget alert that fires when VM spending rises"], "a": [0], "e": "Azure Policy can restrict allowed VM SKUs and deny non-compliant deployments. RBAC controls who can act, not what configuration is allowed."},
{"id": "m26", "d": 3, "q": "A user has Owner on a subscription. An Azure Policy assignment on that subscription denies resources outside approved regions. The user tries to create a VM in an unapproved region. What happens?", "o": ["It succeeds, because the Owner role overrides any policy assigned at its scope", "It's denied, because policy evaluates the resource regardless of the user's role", "It succeeds, but the VM is flagged and must be approved by a policy administrator", "It succeeds, and Azure Policy moves the VM to an approved region automatically"], "a": [1], "e": "Azure Policy evaluates the resource's properties, not the user's permissions. Even an Owner is blocked by a deny policy. RBAC and Policy work together: RBAC decides who can act, Policy decides what is allowed."},
{"id": "m27", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement BEST distinguishes Azure Policy from Azure RBAC?", "o": ["RBAC evaluates resource properties; Policy controls which actions users can take", "Policy evaluates resource properties; RBAC controls which actions users can take", "Both control the same thing, so either can be used for any access or config rule", "Policy only applies to Entra ID objects, while RBAC only applies to virtual machines"], "a": [1], "e": "RBAC controls which users can perform which actions at which scope. Policy ensures resources comply with rules regardless of who creates them."},
{"id": "m28", "d": 3, "q": "A new 'allowed locations' policy with a deny effect is assigned to a subscription that already has 30 resources in disallowed regions. What happens to those existing resources?", "o": ["They're deleted the next time the policy is evaluated", "They're moved to an allowed region during remediation", "They're flagged as non-compliant but keep running", "They're locked as read-only until someone moves them"], "a": [2], "e": "Deny prevents new non-compliant deployments and updates, but existing resources are evaluated and reported as non-compliant. Policy doesn't delete or move them."},
{"id": "m29", "d": 3, "q": "What is an Azure Policy initiative?", "o": ["A single rule that checks one resource property and returns compliant or not", "A set of policy definitions grouped together and assigned toward a broader goal", "A remediation task that deploys missing settings to non-compliant resources", "A role assignment that grants a team permission to create and assign policies"], "a": [1], "e": "An initiative (policy set) groups multiple policy definitions so you can assign and track them together, such as a regulatory compliance standard."},
{"id": "m30", "d": 3, "q": "A company wants Azure Policy to automatically configure diagnostic settings on any new resource that's missing them, and also fix existing ones. Which capability fits BEST?", "o": ["The deny effect, applied at the subscription scope", "The audit effect, with results exported to a workspace", "DeployIfNotExists, plus a remediation task", "A CanNotDelete lock on every resource group"], "a": [2], "e": "DeployIfNotExists (and modify) effects can deploy missing settings for new resources, and remediation tasks apply them to existing non-compliant resources. Deny blocks; audit only reports."},
{"id": "m31", "d": 3, "q": "A team wants to see which resources would violate a proposed rule before blocking anything. Which policy effect fits BEST?", "o": ["Deny", "Audit", "DeployIfNotExists", "Disabled"], "a": [1], "e": "Audit reports non-compliant resources without blocking deployments, which is a safe way to measure impact before switching to deny."},
{"id": "m32", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO scopes can an Azure Policy assignment target? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Management group", "Resource group", "A single tag value", "A Microsoft Entra security group"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Policy can be assigned at management group, subscription, resource group, and (in some cases) resource scope. It applies to resources, not to Entra groups or tags."},
{"id": "m33", "d": 3, "q": "An admin applies a CanNotDelete lock to a resource group. What can users with Contributor rights still do?", "o": ["Delete individual resources inside the group", "Delete the resource group itself", "Read and modify resources in the group", "Remove the lock"], "a": [2], "e": "A CanNotDelete (Delete) lock lets authorized users read and modify resources but not delete them. The lock applies to the group and all resources under it. Contributors can't remove locks."},
{"id": "m34", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about resource locks is TRUE?", "o": ["Locks only apply to users who don't hold the Owner role at that scope", "Locks apply to all users, including Owners, until the lock is removed", "Locks only apply to resources created after the lock was put in place", "Locks can only be applied to individual resources, not groups or subscriptions"], "a": [1], "e": "Locks override permissions: even Owners can't delete a locked resource without first removing the lock. Locks can be set at subscription, resource group, or resource scope and are inherited."},
{"id": "m35", "d": 3, "q": "A ReadOnly lock is applied to a resource group. A Contributor tries to create a new storage account in it. What happens?", "o": ["It succeeds, because a ReadOnly lock only prevents deletes in the group", "It fails, because ReadOnly blocks adding or changing resources in the group", "It succeeds, but the new storage account is created in a read-only state", "It fails only if the new account is in a different region than the group"], "a": [1], "e": "A ReadOnly lock prevents modifications, including adding new resources to a locked resource group. Authorized users can still read."},
{"id": "m36", "d": 3, "q": "Who can create or remove resource locks by default?", "o": ["Any user with Contributor at the scope", "Users with Owner or User Access Administrator", "Only Microsoft support, through a ticket", "Any user with Reader plus a tag exemption"], "a": [1], "e": "Managing locks requires Microsoft.Authorization/locks/* actions, which Owner and User Access Administrator have among built-in roles. Contributor doesn't."},
{"id": "m37", "d": 3, "q": "An admin puts a ReadOnly lock on a storage account. Shortly after, an app that uses account keys stops working. What is the MOST likely reason?", "o": ["ReadOnly locks block all data plane reads and writes to the blobs in the account", "Listing account keys is a POST operation, and the ReadOnly lock blocks it", "ReadOnly locks rotate both account keys automatically when they're applied", "ReadOnly locks turn off public network access for the storage account"], "a": [1], "e": "A ReadOnly lock blocks control plane write operations, and listing keys is a POST action. Apps or tools that fetch keys through Resource Manager fail. This is a well-known side effect of ReadOnly locks."},
{"id": "m38", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO lock levels are available in Azure? (Choose two.)", "o": ["CanNotDelete (Delete)", "ReadOnly", "NoModify", "DenyAll"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Azure has two lock levels: CanNotDelete (Delete) and ReadOnly."},
{"id": "m39", "d": 3, "q": "A team wants to stop accidental deletion of a production SQL database while still letting developers change its settings. What is the simplest control?", "o": ["CanNotDelete lock on the database", "ReadOnly lock on the database", "Azure Policy with an audit effect", "Remove Contributor from all developers"], "a": [0], "e": "A CanNotDelete lock allows changes but blocks deletion. ReadOnly would block setting changes; audit only reports; removing Contributor blocks needed work."},
{"id": "m40", "d": 3, "q": "An engineer wants to manage Azure from a browser on a locked-down laptop with no tools installed, using either Bash or PowerShell with the Azure CLI and Az module already available. Which tool fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Cloud Shell", "Azure Storage Explorer", "Azure Arc", "Azure Advisor"], "a": [0], "e": "Azure Cloud Shell is a browser-based shell with Bash and PowerShell, preinstalled with the Azure CLI, Azure PowerShell, and other tools, and it's authenticated with your Azure account."},
{"id": "m41", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about Azure CLI and Azure PowerShell is TRUE?", "o": ["The CLI only runs on Windows, and Azure PowerShell only runs on Linux or macOS", "Both are cross-platform tools that can manage the same Azure resources", "Only Azure PowerShell can create resources; the Azure CLI is read-only", "Both run only inside Cloud Shell and can't be installed on a local machine"], "a": [1], "e": "Azure CLI and Azure PowerShell are both cross-platform (Windows, macOS, Linux), can be installed locally or used in Cloud Shell, and can perform the same management tasks."},
{"id": "m42", "d": 3, "q": "Which tool does this command come from?\n\naz vm deallocate --resource-group rg-dev --name vm-web01", "o": ["Azure PowerShell", "Azure CLI", "Azure Resource Manager template", "Azure Monitor Logs query"], "a": [1], "e": "Commands starting with 'az' are Azure CLI. Azure PowerShell uses verb-noun cmdlets like Stop-AzVM."},
{"id": "m43", "d": 3, "q": "Which tool does this command come from?\n\nNew-AzResourceGroup -Name rg-prod -Location eastus2", "o": ["Azure CLI", "Azure PowerShell", "Bicep", "Kusto Query Language"], "a": [1], "e": "Verb-Noun cmdlets with the 'Az' prefix are Azure PowerShell (the Az module)."},
{"id": "m44", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about the Azure portal is TRUE?", "o": ["It's the only way to create and configure resources in an Azure subscription", "It's a web-based console to build, manage, and monitor resources, with dashboards", "It's a desktop app that must be installed on a local Windows machine to use", "It's the best tool for repeatable, automated deployments across many subscriptions"], "a": [1], "e": "The portal is a web-based, unified console with dashboards. It's great for exploration and one-off tasks but isn't the best tool for repeatable automation (use scripts or IaC)."},
{"id": "m45", "d": 3, "q": "A company wants to apply Azure Policy, RBAC, and tags to Linux servers running in its own datacenter and in AWS, and see them in the Azure portal. Which service fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Arc-enabled servers", "Azure Migrate", "Azure Site Recovery", "Azure Dedicated Host"], "a": [0], "e": "Azure Arc projects non-Azure servers into Azure Resource Manager so they appear in the portal and can be governed with Policy, RBAC, tags, and Defender for Cloud without moving them."},
{"id": "m46", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO resource types can Azure Arc manage outside of Azure? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Kubernetes clusters", "SQL Server instances", "Microsoft 365 mailboxes", "Physical network switches"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Azure Arc supports servers, Kubernetes clusters, Azure data services (SQL Managed Instance, PostgreSQL), SQL Server, and VMware vSphere and System Center VMM environments. It doesn't manage mailboxes or switches."},
{"id": "m47", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about Azure Arc is TRUE?", "o": ["Arc migrates on-premises servers into native Azure VMs with no downtime", "Arc extends Azure management to resources that stay where they are", "Arc requires an ExpressRoute circuit between the site and Azure to work", "Arc only supports Windows Server machines, not Linux or Kubernetes"], "a": [1], "e": "Arc doesn't move workloads; it extends Azure Resource Manager management and services to them in place. It works over standard outbound internet connectivity and supports Windows and Linux."},
{"id": "m48", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement BEST describes infrastructure as code (IaC)?", "o": ["Writing application code that is deployed to and runs on Azure virtual machines", "Defining infrastructure in code files instead of manual setup, so it's repeatable", "Storing network and infrastructure diagrams in a code repository with version history", "Encrypting infrastructure configuration data at rest with customer-managed keys"], "a": [1], "e": "IaC defines infrastructure in code files (templates, scripts) so it can be versioned, reviewed, and deployed consistently and repeatedly."},
{"id": "m49", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO are examples of declarative IaC tools for Azure? (Choose two.)", "o": ["ARM templates", "Bicep", "Azure Storage Explorer", "Azure Cloud Shell file share"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "ARM templates (JSON) and Bicep (a domain-specific language that compiles to ARM) are declarative IaC. Terraform is another common option. Storage Explorer and the Cloud Shell share aren't IaC."},
{"id": "m50", "d": 3, "q": "What does it mean that ARM templates are declarative?", "o": ["You list each step and command to run, in the order they should execute", "You describe the resources you want, and Resource Manager works out how", "You declare a budget, and Azure picks the cheapest resources that fit it", "You must declare every resource's IP address and dependency manually"], "a": [1], "e": "Declarative templates describe the desired end state. Resource Manager handles ordering, dependencies, and parallel deployment."},
{"id": "m51", "d": 3, "q": "An admin deploys the same ARM template to a resource group twice, with no changes. What happens on the second deployment?", "o": ["A second, duplicate copy of every resource is created next to the first", "Nothing is duplicated; the resources end up in the same state as before", "The deployment fails because the resources in the template already exist", "All resources are deleted and recreated from scratch with the same names"], "a": [1], "e": "ARM template deployments are idempotent: deploying the same template repeatedly results in the same resources in the same state."},
{"id": "m52", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about Azure Resource Manager (ARM) is TRUE?", "o": ["It's only used when deploying templates; portal and CLI actions bypass it", "It's the management layer that handles requests from every tool and API", "It only manages resource groups, while each service manages its own resources", "It's a monitoring service that collects metrics from VMs and other resources"], "a": [1], "e": "Every create, update, and delete request goes through Resource Manager, which authenticates, authorizes (RBAC), enforces policy and locks, and sends the request to the resource provider. That's why results are consistent across tools."},
{"id": "m53", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO benefits does Azure Resource Manager provide? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Consistent management across portal, CLI, PowerShell, and APIs", "Built-in RBAC, tags, and locks applied at management scopes", "Automatic encryption of all customer application code", "Free egress bandwidth for resources it manages"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "ARM gives a consistent management layer, lets you manage resources as a group, and applies access control, tagging, and locks. It doesn't encrypt your app code or make egress free."},
{"id": "m54", "d": 3, "q": "An ARM template deployment includes a VM that depends on a VNet and a storage account that depends on nothing. How does Resource Manager deploy them?", "o": ["Strictly one at a time, in the order they appear in the file", "In parallel where possible, respecting declared dependencies", "Only the first resource in the file is deployed on each run", "Alphabetically by resource name, so the VNet goes before the VM"], "a": [1], "e": "Resource Manager orchestrates deployment, running independent resources in parallel and ordering dependent ones based on dependencies."},
{"id": "m55", "d": 3, "q": "A team wants to see what changes an ARM template will make to existing resources BEFORE deploying it. Which feature fits BEST?", "o": ["What-if operation", "Resource locks", "Azure Advisor", "Template export"], "a": [0], "e": "The what-if operation previews the changes a template deployment would make (create, modify, delete) without deploying."},
{"id": "m56", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about Bicep is TRUE?", "o": ["Bicep is an imperative scripting language, similar to PowerShell", "Bicep is a declarative language that compiles to ARM template JSON", "Bicep can only deploy to on-premises environments through Azure Arc", "Bicep replaces Azure Resource Manager as the Azure deployment engine"], "a": [1], "e": "Bicep is a domain-specific, declarative language with cleaner syntax. It compiles to ARM JSON, and deployments still go through Azure Resource Manager."},
{"id": "m57", "d": 3, "q": "Azure Advisor groups its recommendations into which categories?", "o": ["Cost, Security, Reliability, Operational excellence, and Performance", "Compute, Storage, Networking, Identity, and Data protection categories", "Critical, High, Medium, Low, and Informational severity categories", "Governance, Compliance, Availability, Monitoring, and Automation"], "a": [0], "e": "Advisor recommendations fall into five categories aligned with the Well-Architected Framework: Reliability, Security, Performance, Cost, and Operational excellence."},
{"id": "m58", "d": 3, "q": "A finance manager wants personalized recommendations for resizing or shutting down underused VMs and buying reservations for steady workloads. Which tool fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Advisor", "Azure Pricing Calculator", "Azure Service Health", "Azure Policy"], "a": [0], "e": "Advisor analyzes your deployed resources and usage and gives personalized recommendations, including cost recommendations like right-sizing and reservations."},
{"id": "m59", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about Azure Advisor is TRUE?", "o": ["It applies its recommendations to your resources automatically each week", "It recommends changes, and many can be acted on, but it doesn't enforce them", "It only reviews security settings, not cost, reliability, or performance", "It requires a paid plan before it shows any cost or performance recommendations"], "a": [1], "e": "Advisor recommends; it doesn't enforce. Many recommendations offer quick fixes or links, and Advisor is free. Azure Policy is the tool for enforcement."},
{"id": "m60", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO services can show you that an Azure platform issue is affecting your VMs? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Azure Service Health", "Azure Resource Health", "Azure Pricing Calculator", "Microsoft Purview"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Service Health shows platform issues affecting the services and regions you use; Resource Health shows the health of individual resources and whether a platform event affected them."},
{"id": "m61", "d": 3, "q": "A user wants to see a global view of the health of all Azure services across all regions, without signing in. Which tool fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure status page", "Azure Service Health", "Azure Resource Health", "Azure Monitor Logs"], "a": [0], "e": "The Azure status page is a public, global view of Azure service health. Service Health is a personalized view of services and regions you use; Resource Health is per resource."},
{"id": "m62", "d": 3, "q": "An admin wants to be notified only about outages and planned maintenance affecting the services and regions their subscriptions actually use. Which tool fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure status page", "Azure Service Health alerts", "Azure Advisor", "Azure Activity Log export to storage"], "a": [1], "e": "Service Health is personalized to your subscriptions' services and regions, covering service issues, planned maintenance, health advisories, and security advisories, and supports alerts via action groups."},
{"id": "m63", "d": 3, "q": "A single VM is unreachable. The admin wants to know whether the VM itself is healthy from the platform's point of view and see its health history. Which tool fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure status page", "Azure Service Health", "Azure Resource Health", "Azure Pricing Calculator"], "a": [2], "e": "Resource Health shows the current and past health of an individual resource and whether a platform event caused an issue."},
{"id": "m64", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO event types does Azure Service Health report? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Planned maintenance", "Health advisories", "Budget threshold breaches", "Policy non-compliance"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Service Health covers service issues, planned maintenance, health advisories, and security advisories. Budgets are Cost Management; non-compliance is Azure Policy."},
{"id": "m65", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement BEST describes Azure Monitor?", "o": ["A service that collects performance metrics only from Azure virtual machines", "A platform to collect, analyze, and act on telemetry from Azure and elsewhere", "A cost tracking tool that breaks down monthly invoices by service and tag", "A managed firewall that inspects and logs traffic flowing between VNets"], "a": [1], "e": "Azure Monitor collects metrics and logs from Azure resources, applications, and non-Azure environments, and lets you analyze, visualize, and alert on them."},
{"id": "m66", "d": 3, "q": "An analyst needs to write queries that join VM performance logs with security events to find patterns. Which Azure Monitor capability fits BEST?", "o": ["Log Analytics", "Azure Monitor metrics explorer", "Azure Advisor", "Service Health"], "a": [0], "e": "Log Analytics is the tool in the portal for writing Kusto Query Language (KQL) queries against log data in a Log Analytics workspace, including joins and aggregations."},
{"id": "m67", "d": 3, "q": "What language is this query written in, and where would you run it?\n\nHeartbeat\n| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)\n| summarize LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated) by Computer", "o": ["T-SQL, run in the Azure SQL Database query editor", "Kusto Query Language (KQL), run in Log Analytics", "PowerShell, run in Azure Cloud Shell or a terminal", "Bicep, run as part of an ARM template deployment"], "a": [1], "e": "This is KQL, the query language used in Log Analytics (Azure Monitor Logs). The pipe-based syntax with where and summarize is KQL's style."},
{"id": "m68", "d": 3, "q": "An admin wants to be emailed and have a runbook triggered when a VM's CPU exceeds 90% for 10 minutes. Which TWO components are needed? (Choose two.)", "o": ["An Azure Monitor alert rule", "An action group", "An Azure Policy initiative", "A resource lock"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "An Azure Monitor alert rule defines the condition (metric, threshold, window). An action group defines who's notified and what automated actions run (email, SMS, runbook, webhook, Logic App)."},
{"id": "m69", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO signal types can Azure Monitor alerts be based on? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Metric values", "Log query results", "Resource lock changes only", "Pricing Calculator estimates"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Azure Monitor alerts can be based on metrics, log queries, and activity log events (among others). Pricing estimates aren't telemetry."},
{"id": "m70", "d": 3, "q": "A developer wants to track request rates, response times, failure rates, and dependencies of a web app, and see a live map of how components call each other. Which service fits BEST?", "o": ["Application Insights", "Azure Advisor", "Azure Service Health", "Log Analytics agent on the VM only"], "a": [0], "e": "Application Insights is the application performance monitoring feature of Azure Monitor, with request and dependency tracking, application map, and live metrics."},
{"id": "m71", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO capabilities does Application Insights provide? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Availability tests that ping your app from multiple locations", "Exception and failure tracking with stack traces", "Enforcing allowed VM sizes", "Physical datacenter monitoring for customers"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Application Insights can run availability (synthetic) tests, track exceptions, failures, performance, and user behavior. Enforcing VM sizes is Azure Policy."},
{"id": "m72", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about Application Insights is TRUE?", "o": ["It only works with apps hosted in Azure App Service or Azure Functions", "It can monitor apps hosted in Azure, on-premises, or in other clouds", "It requires a dedicated monitoring VM for each application it watches", "It replaces Log Analytics, so app telemetry is stored outside any workspace"], "a": [1], "e": "Application Insights can monitor apps running anywhere, as long as they can send telemetry. It stores data in a Log Analytics workspace (workspace-based)."},
{"id": "m73", "d": 3, "q": "Where is the record of who created, modified, or deleted resources in a subscription (control plane operations) found?", "o": ["Azure Activity Log", "Application Insights traces", "Azure Advisor recommendations", "Storage account access tier history"], "a": [0], "e": "The Activity Log records subscription-level events, including control plane operations like create, update, delete, and who did them."},
{"id": "m74", "d": 3, "q": "An admin wants a VM to be rightsized based on 30 days of low CPU usage, and wants a single score showing how well the subscription follows best practices. Which TWO Advisor features help? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Cost recommendations", "Advisor score", "Resource locks", "Policy remediation tasks"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Advisor cost recommendations identify underused VMs, and Advisor score summarizes how well resources follow best practices across categories."},
{"id": "m75", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about Azure Service Health and Azure Monitor is TRUE?", "o": ["Service Health is part of Azure Monitor's tools for watching platform health", "Service Health replaces Azure Monitor for any alerts about Azure resources", "Azure Monitor can't create alerts based on Service Health events or advisories", "Service Health only reports on on-premises servers connected through Azure Arc"], "a": [0], "e": "Service Health is integrated into Azure Monitor, and you can create Service Health alerts that use action groups."},
{"id": "m76", "d": 3, "q": "A security team suspects an engineer deleted a production VNet yesterday. Where should they look FIRST?", "o": ["The Azure Activity Log for the subscription", "Application Insights traces for the web app", "The cost analysis view in Cost Management", "Azure Advisor's operational recommendations"], "a": [0], "e": "The Activity Log records control plane operations like deletions, including who initiated them and when."},
{"id": "m77", "d": 3, "q": "A company wants a consistent way to deploy a standard landing zone (resource groups, policies, and role assignments) into every new subscription. Which combination is the MOST appropriate?", "o": ["Bicep or ARM templates deployed by a pipeline, plus Policy at the management group", "Manual portal deployments that follow a detailed checklist documented in a wiki", "Resource locks applied to each new subscription as soon as it has been created", "Azure Advisor recommendations reviewed monthly and applied to each subscription"], "a": [0], "e": "IaC gives repeatable deployments; management group policy ensures every subscription is governed. Manual steps drift; locks and Advisor don't deploy anything."},
{"id": "m78", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about a Log Analytics workspace is TRUE?", "o": ["It stores log data that Azure Monitor collects from many sources", "It stores ARM templates and parameter files used for deployments", "It is a storage account access tier designed for long-term logs", "It must exist in a subscription before Azure Advisor can be used"], "a": [0], "e": "A Log Analytics workspace is the data store for Azure Monitor Logs, collecting data from Azure resources, agents, and other sources."},
{"id": "m79", "d": 3, "q": "What is the main purpose of an action group in Azure Monitor?", "o": ["To group VMs that should scale together based on the same metric", "To define who gets notified and what automation runs when an alert fires", "To group related policy definitions into a single initiative assignment", "To group resources so their costs can be reported together by department"], "a": [1], "e": "Action groups are reusable collections of notification preferences and actions (email, SMS, voice, webhook, runbook, Logic App, ITSM) triggered by alerts."},
{"id": "m80", "d": 3, "q": "An admin wants Cloud Shell sessions to keep files between sessions. What should they do?", "o": ["Nothing; Cloud Shell keeps files in memory forever", "Mount an Azure file share for Cloud Shell storage", "Store files in the Activity Log", "Use a ReadOnly lock on the session"], "a": [1], "e": "Cloud Shell can mount an Azure file share to persist files across sessions. Without it (ephemeral sessions), files don't persist."},
{"id": "m81", "d": 3, "q": "What does this Azure Policy rule do when assigned with this effect?\n\n\"if\": {\n  \"not\": {\n    \"field\": \"location\",\n    \"in\": [\"eastus\", \"eastus2\"]\n  }\n},\n\"then\": { \"effect\": \"deny\" }", "o": ["Blocks new resources in any region other than East US and East US 2", "Blocks new resources in East US and East US 2, but allows other regions", "Moves existing resources into East US or East US 2 during remediation", "Audits resources in other regions but still allows them to be created"], "a": [0], "e": "The rule matches resources whose location is NOT in the list and denies them. Existing resources aren't moved; they show as non-compliant."},
{"id": "m82", "d": 3, "q": "What kind of file is this, and what does it describe?\n\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://schema.management.azure.com/schemas/2019-04-01/deploymentTemplate.json#\",\n  \"resources\": [ { \"type\": \"Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts\", ... } ]\n}", "o": ["An ARM template that declares a storage account to deploy", "An Azure Policy definition that audits storage accounts", "A Bicep file that runs imperative steps", "A KQL query against storage logs"], "a": [0], "e": "The deploymentTemplate schema and resources array identify an ARM template, a declarative JSON file describing resources to deploy."},
{"id": "m83", "d": 3, "q": "A company runs 10 VMs 24x7 and has steady usage for the next three years. Advisor recommends buying reservations. Which statement about reservations is TRUE?", "o": ["Reservations move the VMs onto dedicated host hardware for the full term", "They're a billing discount applied to matching usage; the VMs don't change", "Reservations require each VM to be redeployed so the discount can apply", "Reservations cover the VM's managed disks, so storage stops being billed"], "a": [1], "e": "A reservation is a billing discount applied automatically to matching resource usage. No redeployment or hardware change happens, and disks are billed separately."},
{"id": "m84", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO actions are MOST likely to lower an Azure bill without changing the workload's design? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Deallocate VMs when not in use", "Delete unattached managed disks and unused public IPs", "Add more tags to every resource", "Move all resources to a single resource group"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Deallocating idle VMs and removing orphaned resources directly reduce charges. Tags help you see costs but don't reduce them; resource group layout doesn't affect price."},
{"id": "m85", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about Microsoft Cost Management is TRUE?", "o": ["It only shows costs for the current day, so trends need a separate tool", "It provides cost analysis, budgets, alerts, and recommendations for spend", "It can only be used by the subscription Owner, not by finance or readers", "It's a paid add-on that has to be purchased separately for each subscription"], "a": [1], "e": "Cost Management is included with Azure and provides cost analysis, budgets, alerts, exports, and recommendations, with access controlled by RBAC (for example, Cost Management Reader)."},
{"id": "m86", "d": 3, "q": "A tag is applied as 'env=Prod' on some resources and 'Env=prod' on others. A cost report filters by tag value 'Prod'. Which statement is TRUE?", "o": ["Tag names are case-insensitive, but values are case-sensitive, so 'prod' is missed", "Tag names and values are both case-insensitive, so every resource is included", "Azure merges tags that differ only in case into one tag when costs are reported", "Azure rejects any tag that differs from an existing one only by letter case"], "a": [0], "e": "Tag names are case-insensitive for operations, but tag values are case-sensitive. Inconsistent tagging leads to gaps in reports, which is why tag policies help."},
{"id": "m87", "d": 3, "q": "A company wants all resources in a resource group to automatically carry the group's 'CostCenter' tag, including resources created later. Which approach fits BEST?", "o": ["Assign the built-in 'Inherit a tag from the resource group' policy with remediation", "Apply a ReadOnly lock to the resource group so the tag can't be removed by anyone", "Add the tag to the subscription, since subscription tags flow down to all resources", "Do nothing, since resources inherit their resource group's tags automatically"], "a": [0], "e": "Tags aren't inherited by default. The built-in 'inherit tag' policies (modify effect) add the tag to new resources, and remediation applies it to existing ones."},
{"id": "m88", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about Azure Policy and resource locks is TRUE?", "o": ["Policy blocks deletions of resources, while locks block non-compliant settings", "Policy governs allowed configurations; locks guard resources from delete or change", "They're the same feature, just with different names in the portal and the CLI", "Locks are a type of policy effect, so they're assigned through a policy initiative"], "a": [1], "e": "Policy enforces configuration rules. Locks protect specific resources from deletion (CanNotDelete) or any change (ReadOnly)."},
{"id": "m89", "d": 3, "q": "An admin with Owner rights needs to delete a resource that has a CanNotDelete lock. What must they do?", "o": ["Delete it anyway; Owners bypass locks", "Remove the lock first, then delete the resource", "Open a support ticket so Microsoft deletes it", "Wait for the lock to expire after 30 days"], "a": [1], "e": "Locks apply to everyone, including Owners. An authorized user (Owner or User Access Administrator) must remove the lock first. Locks don't expire."},
{"id": "m90", "d": 3, "q": "A team runs a nightly cleanup script with this command. What will happen if the resource group has a CanNotDelete lock?\n\naz group delete --name rg-sandbox --yes", "o": ["The resource group and its resources are deleted", "The command fails because the lock blocks deletion", "Only resources without locks are deleted", "The lock is removed automatically before deletion"], "a": [1], "e": "A delete lock on the resource group blocks deleting the group (and, through inheritance, its resources). The command fails until the lock is removed."},
{"id": "m91", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about Azure Arc and Microsoft Defender for Cloud is TRUE?", "o": ["Defender for Cloud can't protect any servers that run outside of Azure", "Arc-enabled servers can be protected by Defender for Cloud like Azure VMs", "Azure Arc replaces Defender for Cloud for servers that run outside Azure", "Defender for Cloud requires ExpressRoute to protect on-premises servers"], "a": [1], "e": "Onboarding non-Azure servers with Azure Arc lets Defender for Cloud assess and protect them, alongside Azure VMs."},
{"id": "m92", "d": 3, "q": "A company wants to manage its VMware vSphere VMs on-premises from Azure, including starting and stopping VMs from the Azure portal. Which option fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Arc-enabled VMware vSphere", "Azure Migrate discovery only", "Azure VMware Solution", "Azure Site Recovery"], "a": [0], "e": "Arc-enabled VMware vSphere connects vCenter to Azure so you can manage and perform lifecycle operations on VMs from Azure. Azure VMware Solution runs VMware in Azure; Migrate discovers and moves workloads."},
{"id": "m93", "d": 3, "q": "An ARM template deployment fails partway through because of an invalid property. Which ARM template feature helps catch this kind of error BEFORE deployment starts?", "o": ["Template validation", "Resource locks", "Cost analysis", "Service Health"], "a": [0], "e": "Resource Manager validates templates before deployment, catching many errors early. What-if previews changes. Locks, cost analysis, and Service Health don't validate templates."},
{"id": "m94", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO statements about ARM template modularity are TRUE? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Templates can be broken into smaller, reusable components", "Templates can be linked or nested within other templates", "Templates must contain every resource in a single file", "Templates can't be reused across environments"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "ARM templates support modular files, linked and nested templates, and parameters for reuse across environments."},
{"id": "m95", "d": 3, "q": "A company wants to ensure the same VM configuration is used in dev, test, and prod, with only size and name changing per environment. What is the BEST IaC approach?", "o": ["One template with parameters, plus a parameter file per environment", "Three separate templates, copied from dev and edited by hand for each", "Portal deployments for each environment that follow the same checklist", "A ReadOnly lock on each environment's resource group after it's built"], "a": [0], "e": "Parameterized templates with per-environment parameter files keep configuration consistent and reduce drift. Copies drift; portal deployments aren't repeatable."},
{"id": "m96", "d": 3, "q": "An app team wants to be alerted if their public website stops responding from multiple geographic locations, before users complain. Which feature fits BEST?", "o": ["Application Insights availability tests", "Azure Resource Health", "Azure Advisor cost recommendations", "Azure Activity Log"], "a": [0], "e": "Availability (synthetic) tests in Application Insights probe your endpoint from multiple locations and can trigger alerts."},
{"id": "m97", "d": 3, "q": "A user wants to visualize CPU percentage for 20 VMs over the last 24 hours on a chart and pin it to a dashboard. Which feature fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Monitor metrics explorer", "Azure Advisor", "Microsoft Purview data map", "Pricing Calculator"], "a": [0], "e": "Metrics explorer charts numeric metrics like CPU over time, and charts can be pinned to dashboards."},
{"id": "m98", "d": 3, "q": "Which statement about Azure Advisor and Microsoft Defender for Cloud is TRUE?", "o": ["Advisor security recommendations come from Defender for Cloud", "Advisor and Defender for Cloud don't share any data", "Advisor enforces Defender for Cloud recommendations automatically", "Defender for Cloud only shows cost recommendations"], "a": [0], "e": "Advisor's security category surfaces recommendations from Microsoft Defender for Cloud."},
{"id": "m99", "d": 3, "q": "A team wants to control who can see cost data without letting them manage resources. Which built-in role fits BEST at the subscription scope?", "o": ["Cost Management Reader", "Contributor", "Owner", "User Access Administrator"], "a": [0], "e": "Cost Management Reader can view cost data and configuration (budgets, exports) without managing resources. Contributor and Owner can change resources; User Access Administrator manages access."},
{"id": "m100", "d": 3, "q": "Which TWO tools can deploy an ARM template or Bicep file? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Azure CLI", "Azure PowerShell", "Azure Advisor", "Azure Service Health"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "You can deploy templates with the Azure CLI (az deployment), Azure PowerShell (New-AzResourceGroupDeployment and related cmdlets), the portal, and pipelines. Advisor and Service Health don't deploy."},
{"id": "m101", "d": 3, "q": "A company's subscriptions are spread across several business units. Auditors want proof that all subscriptions meet a regulatory standard, with a compliance dashboard. Which approach fits BEST?", "o": ["Assign a built-in regulatory initiative at a management group and review compliance", "Apply ReadOnly locks to all subscriptions so no one can make non-compliant changes", "Run Azure Advisor in each subscription and export its recommendations for auditors", "Tag every resource with 'compliant=true' and filter the inventory by that tag value"], "a": [0], "e": "Built-in regulatory initiatives assigned at a management group evaluate every subscription below it, with compliance dashboards in Azure Policy and Defender for Cloud. Locks and tags don't prove compliance."},
{"id": "m102", "d": 3, "q": "An admin wants to quickly check all recent operations a specific user performed in a resource group, including failed attempts. Which source is MOST appropriate?", "o": ["The Activity Log, filtered by user and group", "Application Insights live metrics for the app", "Advisor score history for the resource group", "The public Azure status page for the region"], "a": [0], "e": "The Activity Log captures control plane operations, including the caller and status (succeeded or failed), and can be filtered by scope and initiator."}
);
